 with Ampol blaming energy costs, and says AmpolCard holders get 4c/kWh off.\n\n**Ampol buying Evie:** Ampol reported AmpCharge sessions up 116 percent in the first half of 2026. Evie said around 380,000 of the roughly 500,000 EVs on Australian roads are registered in its app (AAP, October 2026).\n\n**Evie:** Prices run 58c at 50 kW, up to 73c at 350 kW. Evie is also trialling contactless tap-and-go payment at select sites, at roughly 5c/kWh extra.\n\n**Chargefox:** About 59–79c/kWh. It's the largest network. Each site owner sets its own prices, and motoring club members get a discount.\n\n**NRMA:** Publicly listed prices are 69, 75 and 79 cents per kWh, with 75 cents the most common. Remote sites cost more; NRMA's own pricing update cites 99 cents per kWh at Erldunda in the Northern Territory. Members have had a flat 5c/kWh discount since August 2025, replacing the old 10 per cent discount. It only applies when paying through the My NRMA app or at an on-site card reader. One October 2026 guide still lists 60–71c with a 10% member discount, which looks out of date.\n\n**Gaps:** None of the results reports a specific price change in November 2026. Older guides from 2025 and early 2026 show lower prices (around 40–60c), so they're probably out of date. Another search, for example \"Evie Networks price change\" or \"Ampol Evie acquisition pricing\", might turn up direct news."],"durationSeconds":10.98153362999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PDktqcfbx4QCgmQcnc93sZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
